    "summary": "The largest rule-level four-ply archetype is r4 undoing itself: 7,870 cycles involving 3,809 distinct post-White positions. In 7,225 of those cycles the bishop alternates between an edge square two king steps from White's king and an interior square one step away. Both states trigger r4, which forces a bishop relocation even when it moves closer to White's king. The second archetype is r4 versus r6 (5,550 cycles): r6 establishes stable bishop protection of the knight and r4 breaks it to separate the bishop from the king. Rule labels identify the last priority that narrows the candidate set; earlier priorities still apply. The separate geometric grouping's largest class has 6,496 cycles and overlaps these rule-level groups. A targeted next experiment would require r4 to make measurable uncluttering progress, rather than forcing a bishop move merely because its starting square triggers the rule. No chess rule was changed in this audit.",
